title: A 4-vCPU, 8 GB machine costs about $34 a month at Hetzner and $48 at DigitalOcean; €7.50 at Contabo buys a bigger 6-vCPU, 12 GB one if you commit to two years; EC2's nearest costs $59, or $41 reserved for a year, with half the cores.
pid: 674ab89f-31ef-49ee-960a-f3e145dd5a5f
body: |2-
  I would try the Hetzner CPX32 in Helsinki for a month (the second
  note above).
  Your table's c7i instances are EC2's compute-optimized line, the
  dearest kind. EC2's burstable ARM line (t4g: cheap, but slowed when
  the processor stays busy for long, which a build does) costs, for a
  2-vCPU, 8 GB machine with a disk and an IP address, about $59 a month,
  or $41 with a year's reservation. Two vCPUs would do (a rebuild took
  45 s on two cores), but the slowing makes it the riskiest choice.
  Hetzner's shared AMD machines cost less ($34 for twice the vCPUs) and
  include an IP address and traffic; their US sites cost twice their European ones.
  Contabo is the price you once paid, with more memory, but its cheap
  vCPUs are known to be shared among many customers, so its speed is
  the least certain.
  As a customer in the US you should pay no European VAT, so the prices
  without it apply: €30.49 at Hetzner, €6.00 at Contabo (not confirmed
  with either).
  EC2's t4g is ARM, untested for skg here; skg's native parts are C
  libraries that cargo compiles, so it should build.
contains:
- e2c0b9c1-7f3e-4871-92a2-f01b2d3f2525
